= Module 1: The South African Laboratory & Emergence in India (1915–1918)

== 1.1 The South African Crucible (1893–1914)
Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi's political philosophy and techniques of mass mobilization were forged in South Africa, where he arrived in 1893 as legal counsel for Dada Abdulla & Co. Over two decades, he led Indian indentured laborers and merchants against racial discrimination.

- *Moderate Phase (1893–1906):* Founded the *Natal Indian Congress* (1894) and published *Indian Opinion* (1903). Inspired by John Ruskin's _Unto This Last_, he founded the *Phoenix Settlement* (1904) near Durban, enshrining the dignity of manual labor.
- *Satyagraha Phase (1906–1914):* Formulated *Satyagraha* (Truth-Force and Active Non-Violence) against the Asiatic Registration Act (1906). Founded *Tolstoy Farm* (1910) near Johannesburg with Hermann Kallenbach. Led the 1913 Great March against the £3 poll tax and invalidation of non-Christian marriages, culminating in the Indian Relief Act of 1914.

#exam-pointer(title: "CUET High-Yield: Core Intellectual Influences")[
  - *John Ruskin:* _Unto This Last_ — Inspired *Sarvodaya* (Welfare of All) and dignity of labor.
  - *Leo Tolstoy:* _The Kingdom of God Is Within You_ — Non-violent resistance to state tyranny.
  - *Henry David Thoreau:* _Civil Disobedience_ — Moral duty to resist unjust laws.
]

== 1.2 Return to India and BHU Speech (1915–1916)
Gandhi returned to India on *9 January 1915* (_Pravasi Bharatiya Divas_). On the advice of his political mentor, *Gopal Krishna Gokhale*, he spent a year touring India by third-class train to observe the masses. In May 1915, he established the *Sabarmati Ashram* in Ahmedabad.

In February 1916, at the opening of the *Banaras Hindu University (BHU)*, Gandhi delivered his first major public address in India. Speaking before princes and Congress elites, he delivered a historic critique: *"There is no salvation for India unless you strip yourselves of this jewellery and hold it in trust for your countrymen... Our salvation can only come through the farmer."* This signaled a radical transition from elite politics to peasant mass mobilization.

