= Module 2: The Pan-Indian Surge: Rowlatt, Jallianwala Bagh & Khilafat

== 2.1 The Rowlatt Act & Satyagraha (1919)
In March 1919, the Imperial Legislative Council enacted the *Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act* (the *Rowlatt Act*), extending wartime emergency curbs. It authorized detention without warrant or trial for up to two years, secret tribunals, and no appeal (*"No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal"*). Non-official Indian members (Jinnah, Malaviya) resigned in protest.

Gandhi organized the *Satyagraha Sabha* in Bombay and proclaimed a nationwide *hartal on 6 April 1919*, inaugurating his first pan-Indian mass campaign.

== 2.2 The Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April 1919)
Agitation peaked in Punjab following the arrest and deportation of local leaders *Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew* and *Dr. Satyapal* on 10 April 1919.

- *The Tragedy:* On *13 April 1919* (Baisakhi), thousands assembled peacefully at *Jallianwala Bagh* in Amritsar. *Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer* blocked the sole narrow exit with troops and opened fire without warning. 1,650 rounds were fired until ammunition was exhausted. Non-official estimates cited over 1,000 dead.
- *National Reaction:* *Rabindranath Tagore* renounced his British *Knighthood*, declaring that "badges of honour make our shame glaring." Gandhi surrendered his *Kaiser-i-Hind* gold medal. The Hunter Committee censured Dyer, but the British House of Lords praised him.

== 2.3 The Khilafat Alliance (1919–1920)
Post-WWI dismemberment of the Ottoman Empire under the Treaty of Sèvres threatened the temporal authority of the *Caliph* (Khalifa). Brothers *Maulana Mohammad Ali* and *Shaukat Ali* (the Ali Brothers) formed the *All-India Khilafat Committee*. Gandhi saw this as an unmatched opportunity to unite Hindus and Muslims, linking the Khilafat and Punjab wrongs with the demand for *Swaraj*.

