= Module 3: Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–1922)

== 3.1 Launch and Program of the Movement
Launched on *1 August 1920* (the day Bal Gangadhar Tilak passed away), the movement was ratified at the Calcutta Special Session (Sept 1920) and Nagpur Annual Session (Dec 1920, resolution moved by *C.R. Das*). Congress altered its goal to attaining *Swaraj through peaceful and legitimate means*.

- *Boycott (Negative Agenda):* Surrender of titles/honours; boycott of courts, government schools/colleges, legislative elections (Act of 1919), and foreign textiles; public bonfires of imported cloth.
- *Constructive (Positive Agenda):* Establishment of national schools (*Jamia Millia Islamia*, *Kashi Vidyapeeth*, *Gujarat Vidyapeeth*); setting up arbitration panchayats; popularising the *Charkha* and *Khadi*; collection of over ₹1 crore in the *Tilak Swaraj Fund*; anti-untouchability and prohibition of liquor.

== 3.2 Regional Dimensions & Subaltern Upsurge
The movement inspired spontaneous peasant, tribal, and labor struggles:
- *Awadh (UP):* Peasant mobilization under *Baba Ramchandra* and the Oudh Kisan Sabha against taluqdars; Madari Pasi's *Eka Movement* (1921).
- *Andhra Hills:* Tribal guerrilla revolt in the Rampa region led by *Alluri Sitarama Raju* against oppressive colonial forest laws.
- *Assam:* Massive strikes across tea plantations with coolies deserting gardens chanting "Gandhi Maharaj ki Jai".
- *Punjab:* Sikh reformers led the *Akali Movement* to liberate gurdwaras from corrupt mahants.

== 3.3 Chauri Chaura & The Historic Trial (1922)
On *4 February 1922*, at *Chauri Chaura* (Gorakhpur district, UP), police fired on a peaceful procession. The enraged crowd torched the police station, killing *22 policemen*. Gandhi suspended the movement immediately via the *Bardoli Resolution* (12 February 1922).

Gandhi was arrested on 10 March 1922 and tried before *Judge C.N. Broomfield* in Ahmedabad. Gandhi pleaded guilty to sedition, declaring disaffection towards the British empire a moral duty. Broomfield sentenced him to 6 years imprisonment with profound respect. Gandhi was released early in 1924 on health grounds.

