== 5.3 Round Table Conferences & The Poona Pact (1931–1932)
1. *First RTC (1930–31):* Chaired by Ramsay MacDonald; boycotted by Congress.
2. *Gandhi-Irwin Pact (Delhi Pact, 5 March 1931):* Release of non-violent prisoners; right to make salt for domestic use; suspension of CDM; Congress agreed to attend the Second RTC. Endorsed at the *Karachi Congress (March 1931)* under Sardar Patel, which also passed landmark resolutions on *Fundamental Rights* and the *National Economic Programme*.
3. *Second RTC (Sept–Dec 1931):* Gandhi attended as sole Congress representative. Rebuffed by imperialists and stalled by communal deadlocks. Returned to India and resumed Civil Disobedience amidst ruthless repression by Viceroy Willingdon.
4. *Communal Award & Poona Pact (1932):* In August 1932, Ramsay MacDonald granted *Separate Electorates to Depressed Classes*. Gandhi undertook a fast unto death in Yerwada Jail on 20 September 1932, viewing separate electorates as a device to split Hindu society.
   - On *24 September 1932*, the *Poona Pact* was signed between *Dr. B.R. Ambedkar* and caste Hindu leaders.
   - Separate electorates were abandoned in favor of *Joint Electorates with Reserved Seats*.
   - Reserved seats in provincial legislatures increased from *71 to 148*. Gandhi subsequently founded the *Harijan Sevak Sangh* and launched the weekly *Harijan* (1933).

