= Module 6: World War II, Cripps Mission & Quit India Movement (1939–1945)

== 6.1 Resignation of Ministries (1939)
Under the *Government of India Act 1935*, Congress had formed ministries in 8 provinces in 1937. On 3 September 1939, Britain declared India a belligerent in WWII without consulting Indian representatives. Congress ministries resigned in protest in Oct–Nov 1939. The Muslim League celebrated *22 December 1939* as the *"Day of Deliverance"*. In October 1940, Gandhi launched *Individual Satyagraha* (*Vinoba Bhave* was the first satyagrahi; *Jawaharlal Nehru* was second).

== 6.2 Cripps Mission (March 1942)
Facing Japanese advances at India's eastern border, British PM Winston Churchill sent cabinet minister *Sir Stafford Cripps* to India. Cripps offered post-war Dominion status, a constitution-making body, and a clause permitting provinces to secede. Congress rejected it for offering post-war promises and enabling partition. Gandhi dismissed it as a *"post-dated cheque on a crashing bank"*.

== 6.3 Quit India Movement (August 1942)
On *8 August 1942*, the AICC met at *Gowalia Tank Ground* in Bombay, ratifying the *Quit India Resolution*. Gandhi issued his clarion call: *"Do or Die"* (*Karo ya Maro*).

- *Operation Zero Hour:* Before dawn on 9 August 1942, all senior leaders were arrested. Gandhi was interned at the Aga Khan Palace (Pune); the Working Committee in Ahmednagar Fort.
- *Spontaneous Surge:* Widespread attacks on railways, telegraph lines, post offices, and police stations.
- *Underground Heroes:* *Jayaprakash Narayan* and Ram Manohar Lohia organized the *Azad Dasta*; *Aruna Asaf Ali* hoisted the tricolor at Gowalia Tank; *Dr. Usha Mehta* operated the clandestine *Secret Congress Radio*.
- *Parallel Governments (Prati Sarkars):*
  - *Ballia (UP):* Under *Chittu Pandey* (August 1942).
  - *Tamluk (Midnapore, Bengal):* *Tamluk Jatiya Sarkar* (1942–44) with its armed corps, the *Vidyut Vahinis*.
  - *Satara (Maharashtra):* *Prati Sarkar* under *Nana Patil* and Y.B. Chavan (lasted until 1945) running people's courts.

---

= Module 7: Decolonization, Partition, Independence & Martyrdom (1946–1948)

== 7.1 Cabinet Mission & Communal Crisis (1946)
In 1946, the British Labour government under Clement Attlee sent the *Cabinet Mission* (Pethick-Lawrence, Cripps, Alexander). It proposed a three-tier federation and provincial grouping, rejecting an independent Pakistan. Following disputes over grouping, the Muslim League launched *Direct Action Day on 16 August 1946*, triggering the *Great Calcutta Killings* and widespread riots.

== 7.2 The "One-Man Boundary Force" & Martyrdom
- *Noakhali & Calcutta Missions:* While leaders negotiated partition, Gandhi walked barefoot through riot-torn *Noakhali* (Nov 1946 – March 1947) and held peace fasts in Calcutta during August–September 1947. Lord Mountbatten saluted him as the *"One-Man Boundary Force"* who kept peace where 55,000 troops failed in Punjab.
- *Final Fast in Delhi (Jan 1948):* Gandhi fasted to restore peace for Delhi's Muslims and demanded India release ₹55 crore in cash balances due to Pakistan under partition terms.
- *Martyrdom:* On *30 January 1948*, on his way to evening prayer at *Birla House* (New Delhi), Gandhi was shot dead by *Nathuram Godse*. Jawaharlal Nehru addressed the bereaved nation: *"The light has gone out of our lives and there is darkness everywhere."*

